<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Calakmul</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kalakmul, Ox Te' Tuun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Calakmul is a Maya archaeological site in the Mexican state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campeche">Campeche</a>, 35 kilometres from the Guatemalan border, and was one of the largest and most powerful Maya cities. It was the seat of the Kaan dynasty, the Kingdom of the Snake, whose emblem glyph is more widely distributed than that of any other Maya city; at its Late Classic height the city had an estimated 50,000 people. Throughout the Classic period it was a rival of Tikal, and under Yuknoom Che'en II, crowned in 636, it held a wide network of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. Its alliances collapsed after Tikal defeated El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in 743 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naranjo">Naranjo</a> in 744, and the city was finally abandoned after the early 10th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/calakmul.jpg" alt="Calakmul" width="330" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PashiX · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pirámides_de_Calakmul.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
